# Global Claude Instructions
#
# Copy this file to ~/.claude/CLAUDE.md to enforce the agent workflow across
# all projects that use this template. Without this, agents are suggestions —
# with it, they are mandatory steps in a defined sequence.
#
# Customize: remove sections for agents your project doesn't use, and add
# project-specific rules at the bottom.
#
# Forge: GitHub (repo, issues, pull requests, Actions, Pages). The template is
# GitLab-first upstream, so a few names keep their upstream spelling: the skills
# /mr, /fix-mr and /mass-merge, and the "pre-MR gate batch". In these
# instructions an MR is a GitHub pull request (PR). Use `gh`, never `glab`, and
# resolve the repository from the origin remote — never hardcode owner/repo.

## First-time setup

When starting a new project with this template:

1. **Run `/init`** — Claude Code analyses the codebase and generates a starter `CLAUDE.md`
   with discovered conventions, build commands, and file structure. Edit the output to
   match your project's actual conventions before committing.
2. **Review auto memory** — run `/memory` after a few sessions to see what Claude has
   learned. Prune stale or incorrect entries; promote durable rules into `CLAUDE.md`.
3. **Verify hooks** — run a quick edit and check that the pre-tool-safety and
   post-edit-checks hooks fire correctly. If they don't, check that the hook scripts are
   executable (`chmod +x .claude/hooks/*.sh`).

## Fast paths by change class — read this first

The mandatory-agent rules below assume the worst case: a new user-visible feature
touching API, UI, and data model at once. **Most changes are smaller.** Apply this
table **before** assuming the full chain. If a change fits one row, run only the gates
in that row — the unnamed gates are out of scope. Do not run them out of habit.

| Change class | Required gates (in order) |
|---|---|
| Pure bugfix with identified root cause | `regression-check` → `test-scaffold` (if no coverage) → `completeness-check` → `changelog` → `/mr` |
| Dependency bump (single package, no API change) | `dependency` → `changelog` → `/mr` |
| Docs-only | `docs` (if a new page) → `completeness-check` → `/mr` |
| Chore / CI config / lint-only | `/mr` |
| Core-logic-only change (parser, calculation, or output generation; no UI change) | `architect` → pre-MR gate batch → `test-scaffold` → `completeness-check` → `changelog` → `/mr` |
| Wiring onto an already-designed surface (a form or toggle bound to *existing* core logic, inside a shell whose design already shipped) | `ux-review` (visual diff) → `security-review` + `regression-check` → `test-scaffold` → `completeness-check` → `changelog` → `/mr` |
| Frontend-only feature (no API change) | `architect` → `ux-design` → implement → `ux-review` + `accessibility` → `test-scaffold` → `completeness-check` → `changelog` → `/mr` |
| New user-visible feature (full stack) | `/voc` → `architect` → `ux-design` → implement → pre-MR gate batch → `ux-review` + `accessibility` → `test-scaffold` → `completeness-check` → `changelog` → `/mr` |
| New trust-boundary subsystem (file import/parsing of untrusted input, generated output another system ingests, external input) | `threat-model` → `architect` → implement → pre-MR gate batch (incl. `security-review`) → `test-scaffold` → `completeness-check` → `changelog` → `/mr` |

When a change spans rows, take the **union**. When genuinely ambiguous, default to the
**heavier** row — but say which row you chose and why, so the user can redirect. When a
change class keeps triggering a gate that finds nothing, that is a signal to add a
carve-out row (see `/kaizen`).

The *wiring* row is such a carve-out: once a shell (a settings area, a dashboard frame) is
designed and its first page has shipped, `architect` and `ux-design` find nothing new on
each further "bind a form to its existing core logic" page. It applies **only** when the
layout is already designed **and** the change introduces no new interaction pattern. A
page that does introduce one takes the Frontend-only row.

## Pre-MR gate batch — run in parallel, not serially

The pre-MR gate cluster — `regression-check`, `security-review`,
`generated-artifact-check` — are **independent reads of the same diff**. None depends
on another's output. Run them as a **single parallel agent batch** (multiple Agent calls in one
message), then consolidate the findings before `/mr`. Serial invocation is the single
biggest avoidable source of pre-PR drag.

Apply only the gates relevant to the diff:
- `security-review` — only if file import/parsing, input handling, output generation, or a dependency's trust surface changed
- `generated-artifact-check` — only if an API surface, exported type, or public schema changed
- `regression-check` — always, on any source-touching branch

**Then, before `git push`, run `completeness-check`** (`.claude/agents/completeness-check.md`)
— one fresh agent that did not write the branch, reading the issue (with comments), the
diff and the code. It is serial *after* the batch on purpose: it audits the branch the
batch's fixes produced. It exists because an upstream audit found gaps in 16 of 20 merged
PRs whose rules were already written down but that nobody but the author had applied
before the push. Fix every finding in a **new commit, never an amend**,
so the fix diff stays separable. Then at most **one** more pass, never a loop: a full
**round 2** (fresh Opus, not shown round 1's findings) when round 1 reported a
`class-missed` or `collateral` BLOCKER or four or more findings; otherwise a narrow
**fix-diff re-check** of the fix commits only, when round 1 returned a BLOCKER or a fix
commit changes executable behavior (application code, CI logic, a gate or check
script — not docs, tests, comments, or changelog text). Record the re-check as
`gate: completeness-check/fix-diff — <N> findings` (`/fix-diff`, not parentheses: the
ledger parser drops a label with a space or paren), `n/a` when the fix round was docs,
tests, comments, or changelog only; record round 2 as a second `completeness-check` line with
`round 2` first in its parenthetical. See `.claude/agents/completeness-check.md`
§ After round 1. Its `## Requirements`
table goes in the PR description, and the `pr-followups` CI job fails any PR description
that names a follow-up without an open issue.

Any Critical/High finding stops the PR: surface it and ask the user how to proceed.

**Record every gate's outcome in the PR's `## Gates` section** — one
`gate: <name> — <N> findings` line per gate run, in the format `/mr` defines. This is
the only record of gate *yield*, and it is what lets a gate that has stopped earning
its slot be found and narrowed. Four rules:

- **`0 findings` is a real outcome — never omit a zero because the line looks like
  noise.** The zeros are the entire signal.
- **`n/a` (out of scope) and `skipped` (applied, user declined) are different
  states.** Do not conflate them.
- **"Applied, but not actually run" has no token — so run it.** A gate marked `n/a`
  with a confident one-line justification is usually an unrun gate whose
  justification was guessed.
- **Never inflate `<N>`.** This ledger exists so gates can be *removed*; padding it
  re-imposes the ceremony the fast-path table strips out.

## Dot-release planning — run `/dotplanning` before development on a new milestone

Before any development starts on a new release milestone, **always invoke
`/dotplanning [version]`**. It is the begin-gate bookend to `/pre-release` (the
end-gate): it resolves the milestone scope, builds the feature→asset map, flags every
missing screen/flow/endpoint/model/doc, surfaces the questions that must be answered
before coding, sequences the work into gated workstreams, and drops a self-contained
HTML report.

- Run it **once** at kickoff — same one-time-gate discipline as `/pre-release full`;
  do not re-run per feature. Re-run only on a deliberate, material scope change.
- It does not run the per-feature gates — it names which gate chain each workstream will
  trigger. Development then follows the plan.
- If the user says "skip dotplanning" or "just start", proceed without it — but note the
  skipped kickoff plan inline.

## Voice of the Customer — run before architect on new features

Before running the architect review on any new user-facing feature, **always run
`/voc all <feature description>`** to get structured feedback from the project
personas defined in `CLAUDE.md` or `.claude/personas.md`.

- This applies to: any feature that changes or adds user-visible behaviour — new UI,
  new API endpoints that users call directly, new configuration options, onboarding flows
- This does **not** apply to: internal refactors, CI config, dependency bumps, pure bug
  fixes with no UX impact
- **What this produces is simulated feedback from modeled personas — not user research.**
  Nobody is asked anything. It is a legitimate pre-launch proxy and an illegitimate
  substitute for a real report. So: never present panel output as customer feedback in
  an issue, PR, ADR, or commit message; if a persona finding is worth filing, file it on
  its merits and say a simulated panel surfaced it. Two panels agreeing is not
  corroboration — only a real user report corroborates a modeled finding.
- **Check for real signal first.** If a real user report already covers the question, it
  decides. Scope the panel to the remainder, or skip it. Never run a panel to argue a
  real report away.
- **The panel average never authorizes a decision and never leaves the VoC context.**
  Hand the architect the key blockers, each with its falsification line, plus the "what
  this panel could not see" open questions — not the score.
- **Seat personas by reachability, not role plausibility.** A persona whose top-three
  daily tasks live on a different surface will mark its own criteria N/A *and* drag the
  average down, producing a low number that means "wrong audience", not "bad feature".
- **After it ships, run `/voc-audit`** against the merged surface: same personas, but
  every finding is verified against the code before anything reaches the tracker, and
  survivors are cross-referenced against existing issues. Once the release reaches real
  users, `/voc-audit --calibrate` scores what the panel predicted against what users
  actually reported. **A release that shipped to users with no calibration entry is a
  `/kaizen` finding** — without calibration the panel is unfalsifiable by construction.
- If the user says "skip VoC" or "just do it", proceed without it — but note the
  skipped review inline

## Gate reference — what fires when

The fast-path table above is authoritative for **which** gates run and in what order. Each
gate is an agent in `.claude/agents/` or a skill in `.claude/skills/`, and the harness
injects every one's `description:` frontmatter into context, so that description is the
source of truth for when to reach for it. This table records only what is **not**
derivable from the gate's own description. A per-gate section restating its description
drifts out of date and costs context every turn.

| Gate | Fires when | What it adds beyond its own description |
|---|---|---|
| `architect` | new feature, changed feature, new endpoint, model change, any work spawned from an issue — **not** a bugfix with a clear root cause, docs, dependency bumps, or no-logic chores | Pass it the `/voc` panel's key blockers, each with its falsification line (**never** the score). Do not implement until its blocking questions are answered by the user or explicitly deferred. |
| `ux-design` | new UI component, page, modal, panel, or flow — **not** backend-only, test-only, docs, or minor styling | Pass it the architect output. The proposal must be specific enough that no layout or interaction decision is left to the implementer. Order: `architect` → `ux-design` → implement → `ux-review`. |
| `ux-review` + `accessibility` | any frontend change that affects the UI, after implementation — **not** backend-only, dependency bumps, test-only, or docs | Run as a pair: `ux-review` checks the design system, `accessibility` checks that real users can operate it (WCAG 2.1 AA). **A new design rule either one surfaces is written into the frontend's `CLAUDE.md` before the branch merges** — left in the transcript, it is lost. |
| `threat-model` | a new subsystem touching auth, authorization, session/sync, tenancy/membership, file handling, payments, or external input | Runs **before** code exists, and feeds its decisions into the architect's ADR. `security-review` is the later, code-level audit. |
| `security-review` | file import/parsing, user-controlled input, generated output another system ingests, auth | Owns the broader OWASP Top 10 and project-specific patterns (unsafe XML/CSV parsing, path traversal, output injection, secrets in logs). |
| `generated-artifact-check` | an API surface, exported type, or public schema changed | Runs in the pre-MR batch. Catches a generated schema, type, or SDK that was not regenerated. |
| `regression-check` | any source-touching branch (not docs-only, test-only, or CI config) | Must come back clean (no stale mocks, no permission regressions, no broken suites) **before** `/mr`, not in parallel with it. |
| `completeness-check` | every source-touching or docs branch, before `git push`, after the batch | Must be a **fresh agent that did not write the branch**. Opus when `batch`'s escalation criteria apply, Sonnet otherwise; record the model on the ledger line. Every BLOCKER and GAP is fixed on the branch or deferred to an **open** issue named in the PR. Fixes go in a new commit (never an amend) so the fix diff stays separable for the `/fix-diff` re-check. |
| `test-scaffold` | a new feature or bug fix with no existing coverage | See *Testing practice* below; that section applies whether or not this agent runs. |
| `dependency` | before adding any package | — |
| `changelog` | any branch touching source (exempt: CI config, dependency bumps, test-only, docs-only) | Fragment is `changelog.d/<issue-or-slug>.<type>.md`, types `added`, `changed`, `fixed`, `security`. **Never edit `CHANGELOG.md` directly.** When in doubt, add one: removing a fragment is cheaper than fixing a red CI run. A PR that genuinely needs none carries the `no-changelog` label — the user's call. |
| `docs` | any change to user-facing documentation | A docs pass is not a claims review. Report which classes it checked (see the agent's own list), never "docs reviewed". |

**Skipping.** If the user says "skip the X review" or "just do it", proceed without it, but
note the skipped gate inline so it lands in the PR's `## Gates` section as `skipped`. Bias
hard toward the fast-path carve-outs: on a bugfix with an identified local root cause,
`architect` reliably finds nothing and costs a round trip.

## Testing practice — always on

These are practice rules, not a gate. They apply on every branch, whichever row of the
fast-path table it took. Do not consider a feature complete without tests.

- **Tests and docs go in the same commit as the code change** — never defer to a
  follow-up. CI is a safety net, not the first net.
- **Run scoped tests before committing** — run the affected test file(s) locally before
  every `git commit`. Run `make typecheck` after any typed language change to catch
  fixture and interface drift. Do not treat CI as the first line of defense.
- **Grep before renaming any symbol, field, or env var** — run `grep -r 'old_name' .`
  across the full repo (CI config, docs, test files) before committing the rename.
  Missing a shadow copy is the most common failure class in batch commits.
- **Grep for changed string literals** across test files when renaming UI copy or
  placeholders — stale assertions fail silently
- **Grep the E2E spec tree before committing any user-visible change** — E2E specs live
  apart from the source and assert on text, ARIA roles, menu items, section counts, and
  key bindings, which makes them the most common CI failure. Unit tests are co-located
  and obvious; E2E specs are not. Update every match in the same commit and run the
  affected spec locally.
- **Watch the new test FAIL before you trust it.** Restore the pre-fix code (copy the
  file aside, never `git stash`), run the test, require red, then put the fix back. A
  test that passes on the unfixed build is not coverage — it is an assertion about the
  author's intent. Say so in the PR: "verified failing without the fix." The vacuous
  shapes that pass review — absence assertions that sample before the action fires, key
  sequences that cancel themselves, name matchers that bind to a neighboring node,
  parameterized guards whose parameter is ignored — are catalogued in the project's
  test-tree `CLAUDE.md`, if it has one (Blueprint-derived projects ship this as
  `tests/CLAUDE.md.example`).
- **Never truncate a mandatory sweep with `head`.** Running the rule and discarding its
  output feels like having checked. And before believing any "0 failures", confirm the
  run reported a non-zero *total*: `zsh` does not word-split an unquoted variable, so a
  batched `pytest $SUITES` collects nothing and the empty result reads as a pass.
- **Treat an intermittent test failure as a bug report, not as noise.** Do not retry it
  away, and do not raise the retry count to make it pass. Most "flaky" tests are real
  races, real stale references, or real crash paths that a user can also hit. See the
  project's test-tree `CLAUDE.md` (`tests/CLAUDE.md.example` in a Blueprint-derived
  project) for the full policy.

## Pre-release audit — use `/pre-release` before cutting a release tag

Before running `/release`, **always invoke `/pre-release full`** to run all agents
across the codebase. This is a **one-time gate**, not an iterative loop:

1. Run `/pre-release full` at feature freeze
2. Fix all blocking findings, triage the rest
3. Run once more as the final check before tagging
4. Proceed to `/release`

## Issues — check for duplicates before creating

Before creating a new issue, **search the tracker for duplicates and partial overlaps**
in open *and* recently closed issues (`gh issue list --state all --search "<key terms>"`),
using two or three phrasings of the problem. `/tracker-hygiene`
sweeps for duplicates after the fact; this check happens before filing.

- This applies to: any new feature request, bug report, or chore ticket
- If a duplicate is found, add a comment to the existing issue rather than opening a new one
- If work genuinely differs, reference the related issue in the new one's description

## Milestone commitment — every issue in a dated milestone carries one `release:*` label

A milestone with a date (a GitHub milestone with a due date) is a promise, and a few
hundred issues against one date is not a plan. State the shape of that promise with one
of three labels per issue. GitHub has no scoped (`key::value`) labels, so **nothing in
the tracker makes these exclusive** — the convention does:

| Label | Meaning |
|---|---|
| `release:committed` | Ships, or the release slips. The **only** issues that appear as bullets in a public roadmap's section for that milestone. |
| `release:reserve` | A slot held against inbound work from real users. Spent when someone real hits something. The reserve is a number written on the milestone, not a sentiment. |
| `release:stretch` | Stays in the milestone and ships if there is time. Moves to the next milestone at feature freeze without discussion. Never a roadmap bullet. |

- **When an issue moves into the current dated milestone — whether you file it
  there, re-milestone it, or a script places it — stop and ask the user which of
  the three applies. Never infer it.** The commitment state is the user's call
  every time: a guessed `release:committed` is a promise nobody made, and a
  guessed `release:stretch` quietly drops work the user meant to ship. If the
  user's own instruction already names the label, apply it without asking. If the
  user does not answer, **leave the issue unlabeled and say so** — an unlabeled
  issue in a dated milestone is a visible gap, a guessed label is an invisible one.
- **Applying one `release:*` label removes the other two in the same call** —
  `gh issue edit <N> --add-label release:committed --remove-label release:reserve,release:stretch`.
  `--add-label` alone leaves the old value in place, so an issue silently holds two.
  Changing an issue's commitment is the same call with the new value; never stack them.
  `/tracker-hygiene` reports any issue that ends up holding two.
- Whatever label the project uses for intended-but-unmilestoned work is not a
  fourth value on this axis. Reason tags (`0.X-hardening` and the like) coexist
  with any of the three.
- A bucket rule is fine for the **first** pass over an existing milestone
  (charter work → committed, other bugs → reserve, polish and unclaimed features
  → stretch). It is not a substitute for asking on each subsequent move.

## PR creation — always use `/mr`

When opening a pull request, **always invoke `/mr`** (the name is kept from upstream; it
opens a GitHub PR with `gh pr create`). This ensures consistent descriptions, pre-flight
checks, and correct heredoc syntax.

- Every PR that resolves a tracked issue **must** include `Closes #NNN` (or `Fixes #NNN`
  / `Resolves #NNN`) in the description body — one line per issue. Without these keywords
  the issue will not auto-close when the PR merges into the default branch. The `/mr`
  skill must emit these lines; verify they are present before submitting.

## Git workflow discipline — always on

- **Never auto-merge after creating a PR** — after `/mr`, stop. Hand the URL to the
  user. Do not call `gh pr merge`, and never pass `--auto`.
- **Never commit or push directly to `main`** — all changes go through branches and PRs
- **Always branch from the default branch** — never from another feature branch
- **Verify branch cleanliness before the first commit** — before committing any work to a
  feature branch, run `git log origin/main..HEAD --oneline`. If any commits unrelated to
  the current feature are present, the branch is dirty: stop, report to the user, and do
  not commit. Never build on top of a branch that contains unrelated work.
- **Always `git fetch origin` before comparing to remote** — never trust a stale local ref
  when the user reports a discrepancy. The remote is authoritative.
- **Check for in-flight work before claiming an issue** — run `scripts/wt list` and check
  for an existing open PR (`gh pr list --search "<issue number>"`) and the `status:wip`
  label. Two sessions picking the same issue is a real and recurring failure, and the cost
  is discovered late, at the second PR.
  `scripts/check-issue-collision.sh` re-enforces this at push time (wired into
  `make pre-push-checks`) because `git checkout -b` bypasses the `wt` claim entirely —
  push is the one chokepoint every branch passes through regardless of how it was made.

### Shell and git traps that destroy work silently

Each of these has cost real work. None announces itself.

- **`git stash` is not worktree-scoped.** `refs/stash` lives in the git *common* dir, so
  every worktree shares one stack: A pushes, B pushes, A pops — and A gets **B's** work,
  which git then *drops* from the stack because the apply was clean. Nothing in either
  session's output names it. Use `scripts/wt stash` (namespaced under
  `refs/wt-stash/<worktree>`). For a one-file detour, copying aside and back is simplest.
- **`git checkout <file>` to undo a one-line probe restores from HEAD** — wiping every
  uncommitted change in that file, including the feature you were writing. Copy the file
  aside and back instead.
- **A pre-commit hook that reformats a file *fails* the commit.** A `;`-chained
  `git commit; git push` then publishes an empty branch at the base commit and prints a
  perfectly normal `* [new branch]`. Assert `git log origin/main..HEAD` is non-empty
  before pushing, and chain with `&&`, never `;`.
- **Never retype a short sha as a full one.** A fabricated 40-char sha fails in two ways
  that each mimic a *different* known failure — a force-push "stale info" rejection, and
  a status poll stuck on "none". Capture shas into variables.
- **A merge command can fail *after* writing the merge.** Every field still reads
  "mergeable"/"open" and the issue-closing keywords never fire; a blind retry mints a
  duplicate empty merge commit. Check whether the commits already landed before retrying.
- **An unmatched glob in `zsh` aborts the line late** — a `;`-chained `git checkout`
  earlier on the line has already run.

### Working with subagents

- Subagents will commit and push without being asked unless told not to. State the
  boundary explicitly in the prompt.
- A silent subagent is usually **stalled, not dead** — resume it with a message rather
  than respawning and losing its context.
- A worktree is **not private**: another local session can commit, push, and open a PR
  from it mid-edit. Before merging, verify local HEAD, the PR's head sha
  (`gh pr view <N> --json headRefOid`), and the sha its checks ran on are the same commit.

### Subagent fan-out ceiling — default for any multi-agent orchestration

`.claude/settings.json`'s deny list guards what agent work *does* (force-push,
auto-merge, secrets). It has no lever for how much agent work one task spawns — that's
this section's job, and it applies whether the orchestration is `/batch` or a mechanism
your project wires in on top of it:

- **Cap concurrent delegated agents at 5 per task**, unless the user asks for more.
  Past that, stop and say why before spawning.
- **A delegated agent does not re-delegate**, unless its own definition explicitly
  declares one level of Phase-1 research delegation (see your project's
  `.claude/agents/*.md`) — and *that* sub-agent must not delegate again. Left unstated,
  an agent told to fix one thing will spawn agents to spawn agents (upstream: a 2 → 16
  fan-out on a task that needed neither).
- **Read-only review agents default to the cheaper model**; escalate only on named
  criteria (unknown root cause, a fix spanning several packages, core-domain semantics,
  or an undecided design call) — not on "this feels important."
- **Explicit override path**: state the larger wave size and the reason in your report
  rather than quietly exceeding the default.

This is enforced by review, not by `settings.json` — a hook that tries to count or block
subagent spawns runs into events that can't block (`SubagentStart`/`SubagentStop`) and a
spawn-tool name that isn't stable across Claude Code versions. Verify what a delegated
agent actually did (`git log origin/main..HEAD`, the PR it opened) instead of trusting
its summary.

## Landing several PRs in one sitting — use `/mass-merge`

Merging a batch one PR at a time reddens the default branch even when every PR's own
checks were green, for two reasons that are not code-quality problems:

- **Aggregate / ratchet gates do not compose.** A gate that counts something across the
  whole tree against a baseline sees only its own PR's tree. Two PRs each under the
  ceiling can be over it combined — visible only on the post-merge run on `main`.
- **Stale merge base.** Each PR is gated against the branch point, not current main.
  Auto-merges on shared files (a generated schema, a route registry, changelog
  fragments) apply cleanly as *text* and break *semantically*.

`/mass-merge` stacks the batch on a local integration branch, re-runs the aggregate
gates after each add, then lands only the safe prefix serially — rebasing each PR onto
the latest default branch and waiting for a green default-branch CI run before the
next merge. It is **user-invoked only**; it force-pushes and merges.

Two rules that apply even when landing PRs by hand:

- **Rebase before each merge.** After A merges: fetch, rebase B onto the default branch,
  run its targeted tests, force-push with `--force-with-lease`, then merge. Never merge
  B on a stale base.
- **Space the merges out.** Landing many quickly exhausts CI runner capacity, and a
  workflow run that fails at *startup* (an invalid workflow file, an exhausted Actions
  quota) reports `failure` with **zero jobs** — it tested nothing. Never conclude anything from one, and never batch-merge to catch up.

## Static analysis that runs after merge — keep local gates ahead of it

If a post-merge scanner (CodeQL, a nightly SonarCloud scan, a scheduled workflow) reports
maintainability findings on the default branch, treat it as a *lagging* signal and keep
the blocking local gates ahead of it:

- Where a lint rule can catch the class outright, enable it. Dead initializers
  (`let x = init` overwritten before any read) are a common example that the usual
  unused-variable rules miss, because the variable *is* read later.
- Where no clean local analog exists, remediate reactively rather than pretending the
  gate exists — and **verify before deleting**. Most "unused symbol" findings on a real
  codebase are false positives: cross-module use, constants referenced from
  configuration, type-only imports under string annotations, or flags read by an
  early-return guard. Grep the whole tree and confirm a symbol is genuinely unreferenced
  before removing it.

## Memory discipline — write for recall, and evict on a rule

Claude Code accumulates project learnings in `~/.claude/projects/<project>/memory/`.
This complements `CLAUDE.md`:

- `CLAUDE.md` — durable rules written by you, committed to git, apply to everyone
- Memory — learnings written by Claude, machine-local, apply to you

The store is **append-mostly by default, and that is the failure mode**. Left alone it
reaches hundreds of files, many with no `description:` and many indexed by nothing.
Nothing in the write path pushes back, so the eviction rule has to be explicit.

**Write it only if a future session would get it wrong without it.** Not "this
happened" — "this is not derivable from the repo, and the obvious assumption is
wrong." A closeout log of a merged batch is not that. The trap you hit inside that
batch is.

**Every file carries `name`, `description`, and `metadata.type`.** `description:` is
what recall ranks on — a file without one is invisible to relevance no matter how good
its contents. It is the only retrieval handle, not optional metadata.

**One fact per file.** If it needs sub-headings for separate findings, it is several
memories. A 2 KB file is normal; an 8 KB file is a session transcript that will never
be recalled as a unit.

### Where a memory goes — decide at write time

| Kind | Destination |
|---|---|
| A durable trap, constraint, or rule | file **+** a `MEMORY.md` line |
| A batch / wave / closeout / audit run | file **+** `MEMORY-archive.md` line — never `MEMORY.md` |
| A per-issue record whose lesson an existing memory already owns | file only; link it from that memory |

`MEMORY.md` is loaded on **every turn**; `MEMORY-archive.md` is not loaded at all.
That split is the whole point. The test for `MEMORY.md` is not "is this true" or "was
this hard work" — it is **"would a session that never opens the file still make the
right call?"** If the index line is a bare pointer (`#123 → #456`, `blueprint`), it
belongs in the archive: nobody can act on a pointer, and it costs context every turn.

Judge by the **hook text, not the filename**. A file named `..._batch_2026` whose hook
states a reusable trap is durable and stays.

### Eviction — three triggers, all cheap

1. **On write.** Before adding a `MEMORY.md` line, check whether an existing memory
   already owns the lesson. Update that file instead of adding a sibling.
2. **On contradiction.** When a change invalidates a memory, fix the memory in the
   same session. Memories are point-in-time and nothing re-validates them, so the only
   moment anyone knows one went stale is the moment they make it stale. Prefer a dated
   **Superseded** note naming the PR over a silent rewrite — the old belief is often
   why a decision looks strange later.
3. **At each release close.** Sweep `MEMORY.md` for entries whose issues all closed and
   whose hooks state no reusable rule; demote them to a dated `## Archived` section in
   the archive. Put it on the release checklist.

Run **`/memory-audit`** at each release close to execute all three triggers at once —
it finds files with no `description:` (invisible to recall), multi-fact files, index
lines that are bare pointers, dead wiki-links, and grounding claims naming things that
no longer exist. A policy with no sweep is a rule that lives only in prose.

**The index has a hard read limit, and truncation is silent.** `MEMORY.md` is loaded on
every turn and cut off past roughly 24.4 KB: the harness loads part of it and says so
once, in a notice nobody is required to read, so indexed memories stop arriving with
nothing failing anywhere. Upstream hit it five times in four weeks. Each hand-compaction
bought 1-2 KB, and the index refilled in about eight days. **Compaction is not the fix;
the entry budget is.** `make memory-check` fails over a 23.8 KB budget or on a dangling
index link. When it fires, run a demotion pass *before* touching the threshold, and
record what the pass yielded — a budget raised instead of looking launders a regression.

**Never delete a memory to shrink the store.** Deleting for size destroys findings that
cost real debugging. The fix for an unreachable memory is a `description:`; the fix for
a noisy index is the archive. Delete only what is *wrong* and not worth recording as
superseded.

### Grounding claims degrade

A memory naming a file, line, flag, skill, or persona records what was true when it was
written. **Verify the referent still exists before recommending on it.** A document
asserting a thing exists is not the thing existing — gates and roster files have
survived in prose for months after being deleted or rewritten.

## Cost discipline — always on

Claude Code sessions can accumulate significant token costs when the prompt cache goes
cold. Apply these by default:

- **Don't idle a live session past the 5-minute cache TTL** — a cache miss on a large
  working context is expensive. If a task needs a longer wait, end the session and resume
  later rather than holding an idle prompt.
- **Brief subagents like a new colleague joining the room** — include file paths, line
  numbers, and the specific question. Terse prompts force the subagent to re-read context
  at full cached-read cost.
- **Prefer `ToolSearch` over speculative tool calls** — only load a tool schema when you
  are about to call it; don't pre-fetch a cluster of definitions "just in case".
- **Re-reading context is the bill, not generation.** Upstream measured a wave of
  delegated agents at 95.9% cache reads, 3.9% cache creation, 0.2% output — so cost is
  `turn count × context size × fan-out`. Default delegated work to Sonnet, paste the
  issue body and file paths into the brief instead of letting the agent rediscover them,
  scope tests to the affected file, and verify an agent's commits before believing its
  summary. One wandering agent cost 25× a focused one. `/batch` encodes all of this.
- **Wait on background work silently.** Once a background task or poll is running, make
  no tool calls until it reports — a placeholder call issued "to do something" between
  notifications is a full-context turn that carries no information. A poll echoes only
  at terminal states, never every tick.

## CI failures — read the log before touching anything

When a CI run fails, fetch the failing job's log (`gh run view <id> --log-failed`) and
identify the root cause **before** attempting any fix. Do not re-run a failing workflow
without a code change unless the failure is clearly infrastructure. Four traps that all
look like "your diff broke it":

- **A green run on the *branch* proves nothing about the PR.** A `pull_request` run tests
  the merge of the branch into the current base, while a `push` run tests the branch tip
  alone, and `gh run list --branch` mixes the two. Read the checks attached to the PR
  itself (`gh pr checks <N>`), at its current head sha.
- **A run can read `success` while a job inside it failed** — any job or step marked
  `continue-on-error: true`. Query the jobs list (`gh run view <id> --json jobs`), not
  just the run conclusion, and pull the artifact: an *error* result (never ran) and a *failure* result (found something) are
  different answers.
- **A red gate that names your code is often a stale base.** The default branch already
  deleted that marker, already shipped the cap the new assertion checks. Fetch and diff
  against the current base before opening the file the gate blamed.
- **A gate whose oracle is outside the repo can red the default branch with no code
  cause** — an API timeout, a tracker label edited by someone else, an advisory
  published after your last green run. Retry the *job*; do not hunt the tree.

A test that starts failing after a rebase may also be the **calendar** — date-seeded
fixtures that roll over at UTC midnight or snap across a weekend. Reproduce on a
pristine default branch before blaming the PR; if it fails there too, the default
branch is latently red.

## Harness self-improvement — run `/kaizen` periodically

The harness is not fixed. Run `/kaizen` on demand (or once per milestone) to audit the
*process itself* — CI, the gate chain, the pre-push hook, PR cycle time — for friction,
and get a small ranked list of speed wins. It is distinct from `/pre-release` (which
audits the code); kaizen audits the harness. It proposes changes and optionally files
tooling issues, but never edits the harness itself — every change lands through a normal
PR. The most common outcome: a gate that keeps firing on a change class and finding
nothing gets a carve-out row added to the *Fast paths by change class* table above.
